        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
A significant number of astronauts suffer from postflight orthostatic hypotension and presyncope. The mandatory use of fluid loading with salt tablets and water, anti-gravity suits, and the liquid cooling garment has not eliminated the problem. A growing body of evidence suggests that there are major physiological systems that become dysfunctional as a result of space flight. The degree of dysfunction varies from minimal to severe. Several studies have provided evidence that autonomic function is impaired during and after space flight. Additional factors, such as local factors, could also be involved. An area that has not been studied in humans is the effects of space flight on nitric oxide physiology and its modulation of blood pressure. The study proposed in this application will continue the pursuit of mechanisms of autonomic dysfunction in presyncopal astronauts. In addition, it will begin to elucidate changes in nitric oxide production and the resulting effects of the cardiovascular system.<br />
<P>The study had two specific aims: 1) to compare preflight to postflight changes in responses of veins to adrenergic agonists between presyncopal and nonpresyncopal astronauts; 2) to compare preflight to postflight changes in nitric oxide levels, inducible nitric oxide synthase messenger RNA and protein, cell adhesion molecules associated with endothelial activation, responses to acetylcholine with and without nitric oxide synthase inhibition, and reactive hyperemia responses in the brachial artery in the arm and the popliteal artery in the leg, between presyncopal and nonpresyncopal astronauts. Presyncopal and nonpresyncopal astronauts were to be defined by their ability to complete a 10-minute upright tilt test on landing day.<br /><p>By determining the mechanisms of orthostatic hypotension in astronauts, new light may be shed on the underlying causes of the clinical situations. An additional benefit is that these studies will add important new knowledge about nitric oxide and blood pressure control before and after space flight. These findings should also sought provide new information regarding the inter-relationships among nitric oxide physiology, autonomic function, and volume regulation in the same subjects.<br />
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
At L-10, R+0, and R+3, a questionnaire detailing food, caffeine, medication consumption, and exercise will be oompleted. Subjects will be instrumented with electrodes on the upper body for continuous recording of ECG. An automatic blood pressure cuff will be placed on one arm. Echocardiographic measurements will be performed. Subjects will remain in a lounge chair. A catheter will be placed in the dorsal hand vein and a specifically-timed protocol followed for infusion of phenylephrine, acetylcholine, indomethacin, N-methyl arginine, and nitroglycerin with measurements of changes in the vein's diameter being taken by Doppler probe over the dorsal hand vein.
<p>
The subject will be moved to a tilt table. Reactive hyperemia responses, which are created by inflating a blood pressure cuff above the arteries in question, will be measured by ultrasound in the brachial artery and popliteal artery. The blood pressure cuff will be placed on the arm that is not used for the dorsal hand vein measurements of the brachial artery. After a baseline diameter is measured, the upper arm cuff is inflated to 40 mmHg above systolic blood pressure and held for five minutes. The cuff is then deflated and measurements will be obtained four times per minute for five minutes. The identical protocol is used for the leg with the subject in a supine position and a blood pressure cuff placed on the thigh, above the knee to measure changes in the popliteal artery.
<p>
A beat-to-beat blood pressure device will be attached to the arm that does not have a blood pressure monitor on it and the arm will be strapped to an attached arm board at heart level. Baseline continuous measures of heart rate, ECG, arterial pressure and aortic blood flow will be recorded for one minute. The subject will then be tilted upright at 80 degrees for 10 minutes while measurements continue. If the subject is unable to tolerate 10 minutes of upright tilt, the tilt table will be placed in the supine position immediately.
<p>
No inflight procedures are required.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
This investigation is no longer planned for flight by NASA; therefore, there are no results available from this experiment.<end></end></p>
